    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the length of a parametric curve described by \\((x,y)(t)\\) from \\(t=0\\) to \\(t=\\pi\\), we will use the following formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nGiven the parametric equations:\n\\[\nx(t) = 2 \\sin(t)\n\\]\n\\[\ny(t) = 2 \\cos(t)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to calculate \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\sin(t)) = 2 \\cos(t)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\cos(t)) = -2 \\sin(t)\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute these derivatives into the arc length formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{(2 \\cos(t))^2 + (-2 \\sin(t))^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the square root:\n\\[\n(2 \\cos(t))^2 + (-2 \\sin(t))^2 = 4 \\cos^2(t) + 4 \\sin^2(t)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 4 (\\cos^2(t) + \\sin^2(t))\n\\]\nUsing the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2(t) + \\sin^2(t) = 1\\):\n\\[\n4 (\\cos^2(t) + \\sin^2(t)) = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the integral becomes:\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4} \\, dt\n\\]\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nEvaluate the integral:\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} dt\n\\]\n\\[\nL = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi}\n\\]\n\\[\nL = 2 \\left( \\pi - 0 \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nL = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nThe length of the parametric curve from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\).",
